  "body": "I saw https://github.com/whatwg/compat/pull/187 right after I checked Safari UAs in a retail store. What a coincidence.\r\n\r\nHere are the devices and their UA strings I checked:\r\n\r\niPhone SE (3rd gen, 2022):\r\n\r\n```\r\nMozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 15_4 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/605.1.15 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/15.4 Mobile/15E148 Safari/604.1\r\n```\r\n\r\niPad mini (6th gen, 2021):\r\n\r\n```\r\nMozilla/5.0 (iPad; CPU OS 15_4 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/605.1.15 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/15.4 Mobile/15E148 Safari/604.1\r\n```\r\n\r\niPad Air (5th gen, 2022):\r\n\r\n```\r\nMozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_15_6) AppleWebKit/605.1.15 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/15.4 Safari/605.1.15\r\n```\r\n\r\niPad Pro 11\" (3rd gen, 2021):\r\n\r\n```\r\nMozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_15_6) AppleWebKit/605.1.15 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/15.4 Safari/605.1.15\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo, like @karlcow said in https://github.com/whatwg/compat/issues/180#issuecomment-1079571138 , Safari on iPad has different configurations depending on their form factors; smaller devices report Mobile-ish strings, larger devices report as if they were Mac.\r\n\r\nIf I recall correctly, iPad changed to report desktop UAs with the introduction of iPadOS. I haven't checked with 12.9\" Pro and 10.2\". But I guess non-mini iPads report desktop-ish strings by default...?\r\n\r\nThere are other things to note:\r\n\r\n* the OS version after `Mac OS X` on larger iPads is `10_15_6`, not `10_15_7` that is what Safari on macOS reports\r\n* the last version token (`Safari/**`) on smaller devices is `Safari/604.1` and is not the same as `<safariVersion>`",
